Lab Sizing
Create a new Ubuntu VM/EC2:
OS: Ubuntu 24.04 LTS
CPU: 4 vCPU
RAM: 8 GB
Disk: 40 GB
WX is containerized and needs significantly more resources than Ansible CLI. The AWX project documentation recommends deployment via the AWX Operator and Kubernetes

Step 1: Build an AWX Server
SSH to the new Ubuntu VM:

ssh ubuntu@awx-server
Update packages:
sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade -y
Install utilities:
sudo apt install curl git wget -y
Step 2: Install K3s
AWX is typically deployed on Kubernetes through the AWX Operator.
Install K3s:
curl -sfL https://get.k3s.io | sh -
Verify K3s is Installed
sudo systemctl status k3s
Test kubectl with sudo
sudo kubectl get nodes
Configure kubectl for your user
•	mkdir -p ~/.kube
•	sudo cp /etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml ~/.kube/config
•	sudo chown $USER:$USER ~/.kube/config
•	sed -i "s/127.0.0.1/$(hostname -I | awk '{print $1}')/g" ~/.kube/config
•	export KUBECONFIG=~/.kube/config
Verify:
sudo kubectl get nodes

Step 3: Install AWX Operator
Create namespace: 
sudo kubectl create namespace awx
Install helm
curl https://raw.githubusercontent.com/helm/helm/main/scripts/get-helm-3 | bash
verify:
which helm
helm version
Install Operator:
helm repo add awx-operator https://ansible-community.github.io/awx-operator-helm/
helm install awx-operator awx-operator/awx-operator -n awx
helm repo update
verify:
helm repo list


Step 1: Verify K3s is healthy
sudo kubectl get nodes

Step 2: Copy K3s kubeconfig to your user
sudo ls -l /etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml
Create kube directory:
mkdir -p ~/.kube
Copy config:
sudo cp /etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml ~/.kube/config
Grant ownership:
sudo chown $USER:$USER ~/.kube/config
Modify localhost reference:
sed -i "s/127.0.0.1/$(hostname -I | awk '{print $1}')/g" ~/.kube/config
Set environment:
export KUBECONFIG=~/.kube/config
Verify:
kubectl get nodes
helm install awx-operator awx-operator/awx-operator -n awx
Check:
sudo kubectl get pods -n awx
Wait until operator pods are Running.

Step 4: Deploy AWX
Create file:
vi awx-demo.yml
Paste:
apiVersion: awx.ansible.com/v1beta1
kind: AWX
metadata:
       name: awx
spec:
     service_type: nodeport
Deploy:
kubectl apply -f awx-demo.yml -n awx
Watch:
kubectl get pods -n awx
This may take several minutes because PostgreSQL and AWX containers are created.

Step 5: Get AWX URL
Check services:
sudo kubectl get svc -n awx
sudo kubectl get pods -n awx
sudo kubectl get awx -n awx

(it may take some time).

Example:
awx-service NodePort 80:32639
Access:
http://<awx-server-ip>:32639

Step 6: Get Admin Password
Retrieve password:
sudo kubectl get secret awx-admin-password \
-n awx \
-o jsonpath="{.data.password}" | base64 -d
Username: admin
Password: <returned password>


Step 7: Login to AWX
You should now see:
Dashboard
Projects
Inventories
Credentials
Templates

Step 8: Create GitHub Project
In AWX:
Resources
→ Projects
→ Add
Name: Windows User Lifecycle Management
SCM Type: Git
Repository: https://github.com/soumendu2001/ansible-windows-user-management.git
Save.
Sync the project.
AWX will pull:
manage_users.yml
vars/users.yml
inventories
From GitHub. AWX projects can sync Ansible content from SCM repositories such as Git
Step 9: Create Inventory
Resources
→ Inventories
→ Create
Name: Windows Inventory
Create host: windows1
Variables: 
ansible_connection: winrm
ansible_port: 5985
ansible_host: <windows-ip>
ansible_winrm_transport: ntlm
ansible_winrm_server_cert_validation: ignore

Step 10: Create Credentials
Resources
→ Credentials
→ Add
Type:
Machine
Username: Administrator
Password: ********
Step 11: Create Job Template
Resources
→ Templates
→ Add Job Template
Configure:
Name:
Windows User Lifecycle

Inventory:
Windows Inventory

Project:
Windows User Lifecycle Management

Playbook:
manage_users.yml

Credential:
Windows Administrator
Step 12: Run Your Existing Playbook
Click:
Launch 
AWX will execute:  manage_users.yml
against Windows exactly like:
ansible-playbook -i inventories/hosts.ini manage_users.yml
but from the AWX UI. AWX provides a web UI, REST API, scheduling, inventory management, credential management, and execution history around standard Ansible automation
Windows Prerequisites Section
Enable-PSRemoting -Force
winrm quickconfig
Enable-NetFirewallRule  -DisplayGroup "Windows Remote Management"
`

Issue 1: K3s Permission Error
Error
kubectl get nodes

error loading config file
/etc/rancher/k
Cause
K3s kubeconfig owned by root.
Resolution
sudo chmod 644 /etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml
echo 'export KUBECONFIG=/etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml' >> ~/.bashrc
source ~/.bashrc

Validation
kubectl get nodes

Expected:
awx Ready

Issue 2: Outdated AWX Operator URL
Error
404 Not Found
while running:
kubectl apply -f \
https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ansible/awx-operator/devel/deploy/awx-operator.yaml
Cause
Deprecated URL. 

Resolution
helm repo add awx-operator \
https://ansible-community.github.io/awx-operator-helm/
helm repo update
helm install awx-operator \
awx-operator/awx-

Issue 3: Inventory Group Missing
Error
Could not match supplied host pattern, ignoring: windows
skipping: no hosts matched
Cause
Playbook:
hosts: windows
Inventory:
windows1
No group named:
windows
Resolution
Create group:
windows
Add host:
windows1
to the group.
Issue 4: Invalid YAML in Host Variables
Error
yaml is not in object format

Cause
Using:
ansible_user=ansible
instead of:
ansible_user: ansible
``
Resolution
ansible_host: x.x.x.x
ansible_connection: winrm
ansible_port: 5985
ansible_winrm_transport: ntlm
ansible_winrm_server_cert_validation: ignore
Issue 5: users.yml Parsing Error
Error:
Syntax Error while loading YAML

Cause
You used:
{% for i in range(1,121) %}
inside a vars file.
Ansible expects pure YAML in: vars/users.yml
Resolution
Use valid YAML:
users:
- name: appuser1
  state: present
Issue 6: Missing state Attribute
Error
'dict object' has no attribute 'state'

Cause
User entries missing:
state:
Resolution
users:
- name: appuser1
  state: present

or modify playbook:
when: item.state | default('present') == 'present'
